window.__CAPM_DATA=(window.__CAPM_DATA||"")+"\\\"/><\\/div>\",\"stemText\":\"A project manager is facilitating a meeting with his project team and key stakeholders to prioritize all product backlog items based on their business value and risk level. Drag and drop each category of items in the right placement in the product backlog:\",\"choices\":[],\"key\":[],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer:<\\/p><div class=\\\"class_s76-0\\\"><img alt=\\\"Source figure\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" src=\\\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\\\"/><\\/div><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\">Items of high priority and high risk should be on top as completing them sooner generates more new knowledge, which eliminates uncertainty and reduces risk. Items of high value and low risk should be tackled next. These items are great for achieving quick wins. The project team can then consider working on low-value and low-risk items. Finally, items of low value and high risk should be put off and placed at the bottom of the product backlog since they are not worth the effort. To sum up, the right order is: A, B, D, and then C from top to bottom.<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer: Items of high priority and high risk should be on top as completing them sooner generates more new knowledge, which eliminates uncertainty and reduces risk. Items of high value and low risk should be tackled next. These items are great for achieving quick wins. The project team can then consider working on low-value and low-risk items. Finally, items of low value and high risk should be put off and placed at the bottom of the product backlog since they are not worth the effort. To sum up, the right order is: A, B, D, and then C from top to bottom.\",\"type\":\"mapping\",\"special\":{\"type\":\"mapping\",\"targets\":[[\"1\",\"Top / highest priority\"],[\"2\",\"Second\"],[\"3\",\"Third\"],[\"4\",\"Bottom / lowest priority\"]],\"options\":[[\"A\",\"A. Items of high value and high risk\"],[\"B\",\"B. Items of high value and low risk\"],[\"C\",\"C. Items of low value and high risk\"],[\"D\",\"D. Items of low value and low risk\"]],\"correct\":{\"1\":\"A\",\"2\":\"B\",\"3\":\"D\",\"4\":\"C\"}},\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null},{\"n\":6,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2X-1\\\">In an Agile project, how many members a project team should include?<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"In an Agile project, how many members a project team should include?\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2Z-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>1 to 5 members<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. 1 to 5 members\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>3 to 9 members<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. 3 to 9 members\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>10 to 15 members<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. 10 to 15 members\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>There is no standard number<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. There is no standard number\"}],\"key\":[\"B\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = B<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">It’s recommended that an agile team should include 3 to 9 members. Since the level of communication deteriorates as the team size increases, Agile organizations favor smaller teams. For instance, it’s better to have two teams of five people than one team of ten.<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = B It’s recommended that an agile team should include 3 to 9 members. Since the level of communication deteriorates as the team size increases, Agile organizations favor smaller teams. For instance, it’s better to have two teams of five people than one team of ten.\",\"type\":\"single\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null},{\"n\":7,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">A project manager is choosing a development approach for a new project. Which of the following reasons would motivate the project manager to opt for an adaptive approach instead of a predictive one?<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"A project manager is choosing a development approach for a new project. Which of the following reasons would motivate the project manager to opt for an adaptive approach instead of a predictive one?\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s82-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Change requests go through the organization’s change control process<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. Change requests go through the organization’s change control process\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Change requests are used for frequent planning<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. Change requests are used for frequent planning\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Change requests are automatically approved<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. Change requests are automatically approved\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Change requests are implemented as soon as they have been received<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. Change requests are implemented as soon as they have been received\"}],\"key\":[\"B\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = B<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">Unlike the predictive approach which requires change requests to go through the organization’s change control process, the adaptive approach welcomes changes and uses them for frequent planning. During each iteration, the team focuses on producing a subset of the product’s features, while continuously refining and reprioritizing the product backlog items to meet new or modified requirements.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Options C &amp; D:<\\/span> Change <span><\\/span>requests are not automatically approved; instead, they are discussed with the product owner first, and then they get prioritized in subsequent iterations as per their recommendations.<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = B Unlike the predictive approach which requires change requests to go through the organization’s change control process, the adaptive approach welcomes changes and uses them for frequent planning. During each iteration, the team focuses on producing a subset of the product’s features, while continuously refining and reprioritizing the product backlog items to meet new or modified requirements. Options C & D: Change requests are not automatically approved; instead, they are discussed with the product owner first, and then they get prioritized in subsequent iterations as per their recommendations.\",\"type\":\"single\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null},{\"n\":8,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2X-1\\\">Which of the following topics can be discussed in a retrospective meeting?<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"Which of the following topics can be discussed in a retrospective meeting?\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2Z-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>How to decompose a complex user story into manageable tasks<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. How to decompose a complex user story into manageable tasks\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Which user stories should be prioritized in the next sprint<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. Which user stories should be prioritized in the next sprint\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>How to fix the regression of a product featur<span><\\/span>e<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. How to fix the regression of a product featur e\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Whether the standup timing is suitable for all team members<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. Whether the standup timing is suitable for all team members\"}],\"key\":[\"D\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = D<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">During the sprint retrospective, the project manager should identify what went well during the sprint and what can be done differently in the next sprint. A possible topic is the timing of the standup. If team members are showing dissatisfaction with the current schedule, then the retrospective meeting is the right event to discuss the issue.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Options A &amp; B:<\\/span> The sprint planning meeting is the right meeting for discussing which tasks should be prioritized in the next sprint and how to decompose user stories into manageable tasks.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option C:<\\/span> Dedicated project team meetings and Backlog refinement meetings should address topics related to product development, including finding ways to fix regressions.<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = D During the sprint retrospective, the project manager should identify what went well during the sprint and what can be done differently in the next sprint. A possible topic is the timing of the standup. If team members are showing dissatisfaction with the current schedule, then the retrospective meeting is the right event to discuss the issue. Options A & B: The sprint planning meeting is the right meeting for discussing which tasks should be prioritized in the next sprint and how to decompose user stories into manageable tasks. Option C: Dedicated project team meetings and Backlog refinement meetings should address topics related to product development, including finding ways to fix regressions.\",\"type\":\"single\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null},{\"n\":9,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2X-1\\\">In an agile development approach, what does a cross-functional team mean?<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"In an agile development approach, what does a cross-functional team mean?\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2Z-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Individuals who collectively determine the best way to accomplish the goal of the sprint<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. Individuals who collectively determine the best way to accomplish the goal of the sprint\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Individuals who take part in guiding the product direction<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. Individuals who take part in guiding the product direction\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Individuals who possess the necessary skills to produce a functioning product<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. Individuals who possess the necessary skills to produce a functioning product\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Individuals who are in charge of authorizing and releasing work assignments<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. Individuals who are in charge of authorizing and releasing work assignments\"}],\"key\":[\"C\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = C<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">A cross-functional team must possess the necessary competencies for creating a product or a service independently, without relying on other members outside of the team.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option A:<\\/span> A self-organizing team is when individuals collectively determine the best way to accomplish the sprint goal.<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = C A cross-functional team must possess the necessary competencies for creating a product or a service independently, without relying on other members outside of the team. Option A: A self-organizing team is when individuals collectively determine the best way to accomplish the sprint goal.\",\"type\":\"single\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null},{\"n\":10,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2X-1\\\">During a sprint planning, a team member noticed some artifacts were missing, and the project manager asked the member to resolve the issue. What should the team member add? (Select two)<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"During a sprint planning, a team member noticed some artifacts were missing, and the project manager asked the member to resolve the issue. What should the team member add? (Select two)\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2Z-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Increments<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. Increments\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Sprint review<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. Sprint review\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Retrospectives<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. Retrospectives\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Daily Scrum<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. Daily Scrum\"},{\"id\":\"E\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>E.<\\/span><span>      <\\/span>Sprint backlog<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"E. Sprint backlog\"}],\"key\":[\"A\",\"E\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = A, E<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s9D-1\\\">An increment is a validated and approved deliverable produced at the end of each sprint, reflecting a functional and usable subset of the project's overall capabilities. It represents a tangible portion of the project's performance that meets the definition of done and can be integrated into the existing product or system. A sprint backlog is a list of tasks selected from the Product Backlog for a specific sprint in Scrum. It represents a subset of work for short-term focus rather than the entire project scope.<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = A, E An increment is a validated and approved deliverable produced at the end of each sprint, reflecting a functional and usable subset of the project's overall capabilities. It represents a tangible portion of the project's performance that meets the definition of done and can be integrated into the existing product or system. A sprint backlog is a list of tasks selected from the Product Backlog for a specific sprint in Scrum. It represents a subset of work for short-term focus rather than the entire project scope.\",\"type\":\"multi\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":2},{\"n\":11,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2X-1\\\">One of the team members asks the project manager when a Sprint is considered completed. What should be the project manager’s response?<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"One of the team members asks the project manager when a Sprint is considered completed. What should be the project manager’s response?\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2Z-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>When all product backlog items are completed<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. When all product backlog items are completed\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>When all the tasks in the sprint are completed<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. When all the tasks in the sprint are completed\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>When the sprint’s defined timebox ends<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. When the sprint’s defined timebox ends\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>When all product backlog items meet the Definition of Done (DoD)<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. When all product backlog items meet the Definition of Done (DoD)\"}],\"key\":[\"C\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = C<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">A sprint is considered complete only when it reaches the end of its duration/timebox, which is usually 1 to 4 weeks. In some cases, the sprint ends without finishing all of the assigned tasks, so this can’t be the criteria for sprint completion.<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = C A sprint is considered complete only when it reaches the end of its duration/timebox, which is usually 1 to 4 weeks. In some cases, the sprint ends without finishing all of the assigned tasks, so this can’t be the criteria for sprint completion.\",\"type\":\"single\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null},{\"n\":12,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2X-1\\\">Using an Agile approach, where should product requirements be documented?<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"Using an Agile approach, where should product requirements be documented?\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2Z-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>In the requirements log<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. In the requirements log\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>In the product backlog<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. In the product backlog\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>In the team charter<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. In the team charter\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>In the WBS<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. In the WBS\"}],\"key\":[\"B\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = B<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">Product requirements are documented under the backlog as user stories. The backlog user stories are then continuously prioritized and refined. The WBS is only used in the predictive approach.<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = B Product requirements are documented under the backlog as user stories. The backlog user stories are then continuously prioritized and refined. The WBS is only used in the predictive approach.\",\"type\":\"single\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null},{\"n\":13,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2X-1\\\">Typically, an agile scrum sprint is ____________ long.<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"Typically, an agile scrum sprint is ____________ long.\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2Z-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>3-5 days<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. 3-5 days\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>1 week<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. 1 week\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>2-4 weeks<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. 2-4 weeks\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>5-8 weeks<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. 5-8 weeks\"}],\"key\":[\"C\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = <span><\\/span>C<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">A Sprint has to be long enough for the team to finish all included stories. As per a Scrum rule, a Sprint should never exceed one month. The duration of a sprint depends on the project size and complexity as well as the team’s capacities. It takes 2 to 4 weeks on average to complete a sprint with a team of 3-9 members working on a single project.<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = C A Sprint has to be long enough for the team to finish all included stories. As per a Scrum rule, a Sprint should never exceed one month. The duration of a sprint depends on the project size and complexity as well as the team’s capacities. It takes 2 to 4 weeks on average to complete a sprint with a team of 3-9 members working on a single project.\",\"type\":\"single\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null},{\"n\":14,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sSU\\\">A cross-functional team should always make sure to focus on<span><\\/span>:<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"A cross-functional team should always make sure to focus on :\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2Z-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Delivering frequently<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. Delivering frequently\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Planning accurately<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. Planning accurately\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Improving quality<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. Improving quality\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Delivering value<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. Delivering value\"}],\"key\":[\"D\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = D<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">Delivering value should always be the Agile team’s top priority as it represents one of the Agile manifesto principles: “working software over comprehensive documentation”. From the client’s perspective, value represents the benefit derived from using a product or a service.<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = D Delivering value should always be the Agile team’s top priority as it represents one of the Agile manifesto principles: “working software over comprehensive documentation”. From the client’s perspective, value represents the benefit derived from using a product or a service.\",\"type\":\"single\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null},{\"n\":15,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2X-1\\\">What is the primary distinction between Agile and Scrum?<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"What is the primary distinction between Agile and Scrum?\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2Z-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Agile is a set of values and principles, while Scrum only presents a set of values.<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. Agile is a set of values and principles, while Scrum only presents a set of values.\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Agile is a set of values, principles, and practices, while Scrum only involves a set of values and principles.<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. Agile is a set of values, principles, and practices, while Scrum only involves a set of values and principles.\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Agile is a set of values and principles, while Scrum represents a set of values, principles, and practices.<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. Agile is a set of values and principles, while Scrum represents a set of values, principles, and practices.\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Agile is a framework, while Scrum is a philosophy.<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. Agile is a framework, while Scrum is a philosophy.\"}],\"key\":[\"C\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = C<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">On the surface, Agile and Scrum look similar as they both rely on an iterative process, frequent client interaction, and collaborative decision-making. The primary distinction between Agile and Scrum is that Agile is a project management philosophy that utilizes a core set of values or principles, while Scrum is a specific Agile practice used to facilitate a project. Although Scrum is an approach within Agile, Agile does not necessarily imply Scrum since Agile encompasses a wide range of approaches. Scrum is based on a small set of core values, principles, and practices (collectively forming the Scrum framework).<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = C On the surface, Agile and Scrum look similar as they both rely on an iterative process, frequent client interaction, and collaborative decision-making. The primary distinction between Agile and Scrum is that Agile is a project management philosophy that utilizes a core set of values or principles, while Scrum is a specific Agile practice used to facilitate a project. Although Scrum is an approach within Agile, Agile does not necessarily imply Scrum since Agile encompasses a wide range of approaches. Scrum is based on a small set of core values, principles, and practices (collectively forming the Scrum framework).\",\"type\":\"single\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null},{\"n\":16,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2X-1\\\">The role of the product owner in an Agile project is: (Select two)<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"The role of the product owner in an Agile project is: (Select two)\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2Z-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Manage the sprint backlog<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. Manage the sprint backlog\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Support the team<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. Support the team\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Manage the product backlog<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. Manage the product backlog\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Represent the business, customer, or end-users<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. Represent the business, customer, or end-users\"},{\"id\":\"E\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2N-0\\\"><span>E.<\\/span><span>      <\\/span>Remove impediments<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"E. Remove impediments\"}],\"key\":[\"C\",\"D\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = C &amp; D<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">The product owner represents the business, customer, or end-user as they’re regarded as the voice of the customer. The Product Owner is responsible for managing the product backlog by maximizing the value produced by the team and ensuring that the stories meet the user’s needs and comply with the Definition of Done (DoD) (PMBOK Guide 7th Edition, page 245).<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option A:<\\/span> The Agile team manages the sprint backlog.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Options B &amp; E:<\\/span> The Agile project manager is responsible for supporting the team and removing impediments.<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = C & D The product owner represents the business, customer, or end-user as they’re regarded as the voice of the customer. The Product Owner is responsible for managing the product backlog by maximizing the value produced by the team and ensuring that the stories meet the user’s needs and comply with the Definition of Done (DoD) (PMBOK Guide 7th Edition, page 245). Option A: The Agile team manages the sprint backlog. Options B & E: The Agile project manager is responsible for supporting the team and removing impediments.\",\"type\":\"multi\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":2},{\"n\":17,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">A key stakeholder requested the project manager to urgently add a new user story to improve the product. How should the project manager respond to this request?<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"A key stakeholder requested the project manager to urgently add a new user story to improve the product. How should the project manager respond to this request?\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s82-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Accept the stakeholder’s request and include the user story in the sprint backlog.<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. Accept the stakeholder’s request and include the user story in the sprint backlog.\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Add the user story to the product backlog and include it in the next sprint to avoid disrupting the team.<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. Add the user story to the product backlog and include it in the next sprint to avoid disrupting the team.\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Add the user story to the product backlog and let the product owner determine its priority.<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. Add the user story to the product backlog and let the product owner determine its priority.\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Decline the stakeholder’s request since it’s the product owner’s responsibility to maintain and refine the product backlog.<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. Decline the stakeholder’s request since it’s the product owner’s responsibility to maintain and refine the product backlog.\"}],\"key\":[\"C\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = C<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">The project manager should add the user story to the product backlog and let the product owner decide its priority. When a new requirement is received, it should be added to the product backlog (not the sprint backlog) and then prioritized by the product owner in order to be implemented. It is the responsibility of the product owner to maintain and refine the product backlog. This being said, a request can be made by any stakeholder and a user story can be created by anyone involved in the project and not just the product owner.<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = C The project manager should add the user story to the product backlog and let the product owner decide its priority. When a new requirement is received, it should be added to the product backlog (not the sprint backlog) and then prioritized by the product owner in order to be implemented. It is the responsibility of the product owner to maintain and refine the product backlog. This being said, a request can be made by any stakeholder and a user story can be created by anyone involved in the project and not just the product owner.\",\"type\":\"single\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null},{\"n\":18,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">To gauge each team member's stance on a particular decision, a project manager uses the Fist of five voting technique. When the team starts voting, one member raises five fingers. What does this mean?<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"To gauge each team member's stance on a particular decision, a project manager uses the Fist of five voting technique. When the team starts voting, one member raises five fingers. What does this mean?\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s82-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>The team member wants to intervene in order to further discuss the decision<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. The team member wants to intervene in order to further discuss the decision\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>The team member is totally against the decision<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. The team member is totally against the decision\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>The team member totally supports the decision<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. The team member totally supports the decision\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>The team member wants to refrain from taking part in the voting<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. The team member wants to refrain from taking part in the voting\"}],\"key\":[\"C\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = C<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">The Fist of five or Fist to five is a voting technique that agile teams use to help them achieve consensus during decision-making meetings or sessions. This method is used as follows: The facilitator states the decision or action the team is going to vote on and then asks each one of the attendees to hold up a number of fingers that corresponds to their level of support for the stated <span><\\/span>decision. A closed fist signifies full objection, whereas five fingers mean full support for the decision. Until everyone holds up three or more fingers, the voting goes through multiple rounds to achieve consensus.<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = C The Fist of five or Fist to five is a voting technique that agile teams use to help them achieve consensus during decision-making meetings or sessions. This method is used as follows: The facilitator states the decision or action the team is going to vote on and then asks each one of the attendees to hold up a number of fingers that corresponds to their level of support for the stated decision. A closed fist signifies full objection, whereas five fingers mean full support for the decision. Until everyone holds up three or more fingers, the voting goes through multiple rounds to achieve consensus.\",\"type\":\"single\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null},{\"n\":19,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sTU-0\\\">Drag four of the following concepts and approaches to their right positions: <span>Agile<\\/span>, <span>Predictive<\\/span>, <span>Hybrid<\\/span>, <span>Scrum<\\/span>, <span>Lean<\\/span>, <span>Kanban<\\/span>.<\\/p><div class=\\\"class_s76-0\\\" id=\\\"page_31\\\"><img alt=\\\"Source figure\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" src=\\\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\\\"/><\\/div>\",\"stemText\":\"Drag four of the following concepts and approaches to their right positions: Agile , Predictive , Hybrid , Scrum , Lean , Kanban .\",\"choices\":[],\"key\":[],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sBZ-0\\\">Correct Answer:<\\/p><div class=\\\"class_sXN-0\\\"><img alt=\\\"Source figure\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" src=\\\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\\\"/><\\/div><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\">A. Lean: a set of concepts that includes eliminating waste, amplifying learning, and delivering as fast as possible.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">B. Kanban: a subset of lean which also refers to a framework within agile.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">C. Agile: a subset of lean and an umbrella for several frameworks.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">D. Scrum: one of the agile frameworks.<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer: A. Lean: a set of concepts that includes eliminating waste, amplifying learning, and delivering as fast as possible. B. Kanban: a subset of lean which also refers to a framework within agile. C. Agile: a subset of lean and an umbrella for several frameworks. D. Scrum: one of the agile frameworks.\",\"type\":\"mapping\",\"special\":{\"type\":\"mapping\",\"targets\":[[\"A\",\"A\"],[\"B\",\"B\"],[\"C\",\"C\"],[\"D\",\"D\"]],\"options\":[[\"Agile\",\"Agile\"],[\"Predictive\",\"Predictive\"],[\"Hybrid\",\"Hybrid\"],[\"Scrum\",\"Scrum\"],[\"Lean\",\"Lean\"],[\"Kanban\",\"Kanban\"]],\"correct\":{\"A\":\"Lean\",\"B\":\"Kanban\",\"C\":\"Agile\",\"D\":\"Scrum\"}},\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null},{\"n\":20,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">What is a key element of delivering value early in Agile projects?<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"What is a key element of delivering value early in Agile projects?\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s82-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Detailed upfront planning<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. Detailed upfront planning\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>High-frequency documentation<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. High-frequency documentation\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Small, frequent deliveries of working products<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. Small, frequent deliveries of working products\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Single-phase development<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. Single-phase development\"}],\"key\":[\"C\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = C<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">Agile methodologies emphasize delivering small, working increments of a product frequently. This allows teams to obtain feedback, adapt, and make improvements while delivering value continuously.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option A:<\\/span> Agile favors adaptive planning over detailed upfront planning. In Agile, planning happens in iterations rather than fully at the beginning of the project, allowing flexibility as the project evolves.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option B:<\\/span> Agile values \\\"working software over comprehensive documentation\\\" as per the Agile Manifesto. The focus is on delivering functional products rather than spending too much time on documentation.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option D:<\\/span> Agile approaches use iterative and incremental methods rather than completing the project in a single phase.<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = C Agile methodologies emphasize delivering small, working increments of a product frequently. This allows teams to obtain feedback, adapt, and make improvements while delivering value continuously. Option A: Agile favors adaptive planning over detailed upfront planning. In Agile, planning happens in iterations rather than fully at the beginning of the project, allowing flexibility as the project evolves. Option B: Agile values \\\"working software over comprehensive documentation\\\" as per the Agile Manifesto. The focus is on delivering functional products rather than spending too much time on documentation. Option D: Agile approaches use iterative and incremental methods rather than completing the project in a single phase.\",\"type\":\"single\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null},{\"n\":21,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">What is a key factor that helps agile teams deliver at maximum speed?<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"What is a key factor that helps agile teams deliver at maximum speed?\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s82-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Multitasking<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. Multitasking\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Continuous integration<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. Continuous integration\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Formal sign-offs<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. Formal sign-offs\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Strict risk mitigation<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. Strict risk mitigation\"}],\"key\":[\"B\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = B<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">Continuous integration involves the frequent incorporation of individual contributions into the overall project and retesting the entire product to ensure that the integration is successful. (Agile Practice Guide, page 56)<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option A:<\\/span> Multitasking typically slows down progress. Agile teams aim for focused work by limiting work in progress and encouraging collaboration rather than splitting focus among multiple tasks.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option C:<\\/span> Agile methodologies emphasize lightweight processes, continuous feedback, and flexibility rather than formal sign-offs. Formal sign-offs can introduce delays as they often involve hierarchical approvals.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option D:<\\/span> Agile focuses on iterative development, feedback, and adaptation to mitigate risks rather than strict, upfront risk management.<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = B Continuous integration involves the frequent incorporation of individual contributions into the overall project and retesting the entire product to ensure that the integration is successful. (Agile Practice Guide, page 56) Option A: Multitasking typically slows down progress. Agile teams aim for focused work by limiting work in progress and encouraging collaboration rather than splitting focus among multiple tasks. Option C: Agile methodologies emphasize lightweight processes, continuous feedback, and flexibility rather than formal sign-offs. Formal sign-offs can introduce delays as they often involve hierarchical approvals. Option D: Agile focuses on iterative development, feedback, and adaptation to mitigate risks rather than strict, upfront risk management.\",\"type\":\"single\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null},{\"n\":22,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">How does scope management in adaptive projects impact the creation of WBS?<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"How does scope management in adaptive projects impact the creation of WBS?\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s82-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>WBS is replaced by ongoing backlog refinement<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. WBS is replaced by ongoing backlog refinement\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Detailed WBS is created at the start<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. Detailed WBS is created at the start\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>WBS is static and not modified<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. WBS is static and not modified\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>An accurate WBS is created at the end of the project<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. An accurate WBS is created at the end of the project\"}],\"key\":[\"A\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = A<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">In agile projects, scope evolves continuously. Rather than creating a static Work Breakdown Structure (WBS) at the start, the scope is managed through the backlog, which is refined continuously.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option B:<\\/span> Creating a detailed WBS at the start is typical in a predictive project. Agile projects, on the other hand, favor ongoing backlog refinement rather than upfront detailed scope management.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option D:<\\/span> Agile projects focus on delivering increments of value throughout the project. Waiting until project closure to define scope contradicts Agile's iterative delivery approach.<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = A In agile projects, scope evolves continuously. Rather than creating a static Work Breakdown Structure (WBS) at the start, the scope is managed through the backlog, which is refined continuously. Option B: Creating a detailed WBS at the start is typical in a predictive project. Agile projects, on the other hand, favor ongoing backlog refinement rather than upfront detailed scope management. Option D: Agile projects focus on delivering increments of value throughout the project. Waiting until project closure to define scope contradicts Agile's iterative delivery approach.\",\"type\":\"single\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null},{\"n\":23,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">What is the key measure of progress in iteration-based agile?<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"What is the key measure of progress in iteration-based agile?\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s82-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Lead tim<span><\\/span>e<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. Lead tim e\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Cycle time<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. Cycle time\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Completed iterations<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. Completed iterations\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Completed work items at the end of each iteration<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. Completed work items at the end of each iteration\"}],\"key\":[\"D\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = D<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">In iteration-based agile, progress is measured primarily by the number of work items or features that are completed at the end of each iteration. This approach aligns with the Agile Manifesto's principle that \\\"working software is the primary measure of progress.\\\"<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option A:<\\/span> Lead time measures the time from when a feature is requested to when it is delivered.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option B:<\\/span> Cycle time refers to the duration it takes to complete an individual task from start to finish.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option C:<\\/span> While completing iterations is important, the focus in agile is on delivering working products or features.<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = D In iteration-based agile, progress is measured primarily by the number of work items or features that are completed at the end of each iteration. This approach aligns with the Agile Manifesto's principle that \\\"working software is the primary measure of progress.\\\" Option A: Lead time measures the time from when a feature is requested to when it is delivered. Option B: Cycle time refers to the duration it takes to complete an individual task from start to finish. Option C: While completing iterations is important, the focus in agile is on delivering working products or features.\",\"type\":\"single\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null},{\"n\":24,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">How is work-in-progress managed differently in flow-based agile compared to iteration-based agile?<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"How is work-in-progress managed differently in flow-based agile compared to iteration-based agile?\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s82-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>WIP is the same in both approaches<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. WIP is the same in both approaches\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Flow-based agile uses WIP limits to manage tasks<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. Flow-based agile uses WIP limits to manage tasks\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Iteration-based agile uses columns to manage work<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. Iteration-based agile uses columns to manage work\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Flow-based agile does not manage WIP<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. Flow-based agile does not manage WIP\"}],\"key\":[\"B\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = B<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">In flow-based agile, such as Kanban, teams manage work by limiting the number of tasks that can be in progress at any given time.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option A:<\\/span> Iteration-based agile does not use explicit WIP limits but focuses on completing a set of tasks within an iteratio<span><\\/span>n<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option C:<\\/span> While iteration-based agile can use columns on task boards, it primarily focuses on completing a defined scope within an iteration.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option D:<\\/span> Flow-based agile, especially in Kanban, manages work explicitly through WIP limits to maintain flow and reduce overload.<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = B In flow-based agile, such as Kanban, teams manage work by limiting the number of tasks that can be in progress at any given time. Option A: Iteration-based agile does not use explicit WIP limits but focuses on completing a set of tasks within an iteratio n Option C: While iteration-based agile can use columns on task boards, it primarily focuses on completing a defined scope within an iteration. Option D: Flow-based agile, especially in Kanban, manages work explicitly through WIP limits to maintain flow and reduce overload.\",\"type\":\"single\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null},{\"n\":25,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">In which methodology does the team continuously work on the highest priority item, without the use of timeboxed iterations?<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"In which methodology does the team continuously work on the highest priority item, without the use of timeboxed iterations?\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s82-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Scrumban<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. Scrumban\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>XP<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. XP\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Kanban<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. Kanban\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Scrum<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. Scrum\"}],\"key\":[\"C\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = C<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">In Kanban, there are no fixed timeboxed iterations like in Scrum or XP. Teams continuously work on the highest priority items from a backlog, with a focus on limiting work in progress (WIP) and improving flow.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option A:<\\/span> Scrumban combines elements of Scrum and Kanban, it retains some Scrum practices like timeboxed iterations, especially for planning or retrospectives.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option B:<\\/span> XP uses timeboxed iterations and emphasizes frequent releases and iterations.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option D:<\\/span> Scrum is centered around fixed-length iterations called sprints, which are always timeboxed (usually 2-4 weeks).<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = C In Kanban, there are no fixed timeboxed iterations like in Scrum or XP. Teams continuously work on the highest priority items from a backlog, with a focus on limiting work in progress (WIP) and improving flow. Option A: Scrumban combines elements of Scrum and Kanban, it retains some Scrum practices like timeboxed iterations, especially for planning or retrospectives. Option B: XP uses timeboxed iterations and emphasizes frequent releases and iterations. Option D: Scrum is centered around fixed-length iterations called sprints, which are always timeboxed (usually 2-4 weeks).\",\"type\":\"single\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null}]},{\"id\":\"business-analysis\",\"title\":\"Business Analysis Frameworks\",\"kind\":\"Domain Practice\",\"count\":25,\"questions\":[{\"n\":1,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">The project sponsor expressed concerns to the project manager about whether the resulting product would meet business needs. Therefore, the project sponsor is looking to keep costs to a minimum. What should the project manager do?<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"The project sponsor expressed concerns to the project manager about whether the resulting product would meet business needs. Therefore, the project sponsor is looking to keep costs to a minimum. What should the project manager do?\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s82-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Identify and implement the requirements for making a Minimum Viable Product (MVP).<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. Identify and implement the requirements for making a Minimum Viable Product (MVP).\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Gather all requirements and execute the project using an incremental approach.<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. Gather all requirements and execute the project using an incremental approach.\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Exclude certain stakeholders from the scope definition process in order to limit requirements.<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. Exclude certain stakeholders from the scope definition process in order to limit requirements.\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Sign with the sponsor a fixed-cost contract to limit project costs.<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. Sign with the sponsor a fixed-cost contract to limit project costs.\"}],\"key\":[\"A\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = A<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">The Minimum Viable Product (MVP) is used to define the scope of the first release by identifying the requirements that would deliver value to customers (PMBOK Guide 7th Edition, page 243).<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option B:<\\/span> An incremental approach is not suitable for the described scenario since the client won’t get a usable product until the end of the project. It’s not appropriate to exclude certain stakeholders in order to limit requirements.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option C:<\\/span> The project manager should involve all stakeholders in the process of collecting and prioritizing product features.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option D:<\\/span> A fixed-cost contract will not solve the problem in the described scenario since the main concern of the sponsor is verifying the product’s business demand.<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = A The Minimum Viable Product (MVP) is used to define the scope of the first release by identifying the requirements that would deliver value to customers (PMBOK Guide 7th Edition, page 243). Option B: An incremental approach is not suitable for the described scenario since the client won’t get a usable product until the end of the project. It’s not appropriate to exclude certain stakeholders in order to limit requirements. Option C: The project manager should involve all stakeholders in the process of collecting and prioritizing product features. Option D: A fixed-cost contract will not solve the problem in the described scenario since the main concern of the sponsor is verifying the product’s business demand.\",\"type\":\"single\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null},{\"n\":2,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">A project manager determines viable options and provides recommendations about the investment efficiency of two potential projects by implementing valuation techniques. Knowing that the discount rate is unknown, what should the project manager do?<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"A project manager determines viable options and provides recommendations about the investment efficiency of two potential projects by implementing valuation techniques. Knowing that the discount rate is unknown, what should the project manager do?\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s82-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Choose the project that has the highest ROI<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. Choose the project that has the highest ROI\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Choose the project that has the lowest ROI<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. Choose the project that has the lowest ROI\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Choose the project that has the highest NPV<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. Choose the project that has the highest NPV\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Choose the project that has the lowest NPV<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. Choose the project that has the lowest NPV\"}],\"key\":[\"A\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = A<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">Calculating a project’s Net Present Value (NPV) requires knowing its discount rate (i):<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">Net Present Value (NPV) = Future payment (F) / [ (1 + Discount rate (i) ) ^ number of periods in the future the cash flow is (n) ]. Since this is not an option in the described scenario, the project manager should rely on the Return On Investment (ROI) and pick the project with the highest value. (The PMI Guide to Business Analysis, page 90)<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = A Calculating a project’s Net Present Value (NPV) requires knowing its discount rate (i): Net Present Value (NPV) = Future payment (F) / [ (1 + Discount rate (i) ) ^ number of periods in the future the cash flow is (n) ]. Since this is not an option in the described scenario, the project manager should rely on the Return On Investment (ROI) and pick the project with the highest value. (The PMI Guide to Business Analysis, page 90)\",\"type\":\"single\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null},{\"n\":3,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">An organization is considering two potential projects with identical payback periods. After conducting a benefit measurement analysis, the project manager discovers that Project B has a higher Internal Rate of Return (IRR) than Project A. Which project should the organization select?<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"An organization is considering two potential projects with identical payback periods. After conducting a benefit measurement analysis, the project manager discovers that Project B has a higher Internal Rate of Return (IRR) than Project A. Which project should the organization select?\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s82-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Project A<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. Project A\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Project B<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. Project B\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>There is no difference<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. There is no difference\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>The available information is not enough to make a decision<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. The available information is not enough to make a decision\"}],\"key\":[\"B\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = B<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">The Internal Rate of Return is the annual rate of growth a project is expected to generate. The higher the IRR, the more desirable an investment is. Project B has a higher IRR, thus it will obviously be selected over Project A.<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = B The Internal Rate of Return is the annual rate of growth a project is expected to generate. The higher the IRR, the more desirable an investment is. Project B has a higher IRR, thus it will obviously be selected over Project A.\",\"type\":\"single\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null},{\"n\":4,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">The customer sends the project manager a document outlining the new project’s requirements. While checking the document, the project manager struggles to understand some of the requirements, outputs, and especially the ultimate goal of the project. What should the project manager do next?<\\/p>\",\"stemText\":\"The customer sends the project manager a document outlining the new project’s requirements. While checking the document, the project manager struggles to understand some of the requirements, outputs, and especially the ultimate goal of the project. What should the project manager do next?\",\"choices\":[{\"id\":\"A\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s82-0\\\"><span>A.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Ask the customer for clarification<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"A. Ask the customer for clarification\"},{\"id\":\"B\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>B.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Reject the new project as it involves a lot of ambiguity<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"B. Reject the new project as it involves a lot of ambiguity\"},{\"id\":\"C\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>C.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Start planning the first iteration<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"C. Start planning the first iteration\"},{\"id\":\"D\",\"html\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sR-0\\\"><span>D.<\\/span><span>     <\\/span>Acquire a team to help analyze the project requirements<\\/p>\",\"text\":\"D. Acquire a team to help analyze the project requirements\"}],\"key\":[\"A\"],\"answerHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_s9B-0\\\">Correct Answer = A<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s2J-0\\\">In case of ambiguity, the project manager should first ask for clarifications from the customer before making any further decisions.<\\/p><p class=\\\"class_s80-1\\\"><span>Option C:<\\/span> The project involves unclear requirements and objectives. Thus, it’s not appropriate to start planning project work or acquire a team without first creating a clear vision of what the project consists of.<\\/p>\",\"answerText\":\"Correct Answer = A In case of ambiguity, the project manager should first ask for clarifications from the customer before making any further decisions. Option C: The project involves unclear requirements and objectives. Thus, it’s not appropriate to start planning project work or acquire a team without first creating a clear vision of what the project consists of.\",\"type\":\"single\",\"special\":null,\"manual\":false,\"sourceIssue\":\"\",\"selectCount\":null},{\"n\":5,\"stemHtml\":\"<p class=\\\"class_sFM-1\\\">To guide the development team on what they intend to achieve, a product owner provides a table that includes the key deliverables of each quarter of the 9-month project. What artifact does this table represent?<\\/p><div class=\\\"class_sFS-0\\\" id=\\\"page_41\\\"><img alt=\\\"Source figure\\\" loading=\\\"lazy\\\" src=\\\"data:image/jpeg;base64,";
